{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793188860,"nonce":"LZPHoVcvffrbmH5Zzfkv7gwdSEM=","message":"Release investigation, 2026-10-28: StableNodeId already derives from build path/key and resolves through each RenderTree. Identical named roots in separate trees intentionally have equal stable IDs and equal local slotmap IDs. Stable continuity is retained; replacing it with process-wide live node allocation was rejected. That experiment required sparse hashing on layout lookups (2M isolated lookups about 1.7ms versus 9.6ms), and would require rebasing retained state maps. It was removed before final implementation.\n\nBaseline runtime negatives: a secondary opacity binding made the primary root 0.4 instead of 0.2; queued writes survived tree drop. A separate offscreen regression showed a shared renderer presenting the secondary blue background for a primary red frame (direct frame 2). Logs /private/tmp/blinc-tree-isolation-negative.log and /private/tmp/blinc-tree-scope-cache-negative.log.\n\nImplementation retains SlotMap and stable-ID hashes. LayoutTree has a lifetime namespace; property/subtree records and binding registries use it. Build/event/paint/canvas callbacks establish the owner, region/base-updater tables are scoped, old queues are discarded on drop/replacement, and secondary frames drain their own updates. Renderer batch/static/promoted caches reset on owner changes. Legacy unscoped low-level producers remain available; custom multi-tree producers can use RenderTree::with_property_updates or set a record tree_scope explicitly. The new public queue-record field is a Rust API change for struct-literal callers.\n\nCurrent normal gates passed: 115 layout tests (11 new isolation, 101 related property/binding/region/class/ref/state-layer controls, three existing keyed-stable-ID controls) and 27 app tests (one new offscreen window/cache/canvas test, eight mixed rendering boundaries, eight 